# Trivy exceptions.
#
# Two entries, each with a reason. Nothing is suppressed because it was
# inconvenient: a scanner finding that gets silenced without an argument is
# worse than no scanner, because the next person assumes it was considered.
#
# The third HIGH trivy raised, AWS-0345 (IAM role allows s3:*), is NOT here.
# That one was a real over-permission and the policy was narrowed instead. See
# modules/ci-oidc/main.tf.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0132 — "Bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key"
#
# The buckets use SSE-S3 (AES256), which is server-side encryption at rest with
# keys AWS manages. Trivy wants SSE-KMS with a customer-managed key.
#
# A CMK costs $1/month per key plus per-request charges. This stack's entire
# steady-state cost is about $0.55/month, so a CMK would roughly triple it.
#
# What is in these buckets: the compiled output of a public website, and
# Terraform state describing that website. The site content is served
# unauthenticated to anyone who visits, so encrypting it with a customer-managed
# key protects nothing that is not already public. State is more sensitive, and
# it holds resource ARNs and an account id rather than credentials, with public
# access blocked on all four settings and bucket-owner-enforced ownership.
#
# The threat a CMK actually defends against is an AWS-internal compromise of the
# managed key hierarchy. That is not this project's threat model.
AWS-0132

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0011 — "Distribution does not utilize a WAF"
#
# AWS WAF is about $5/month for the web ACL, plus per-rule and per-request
# charges. That is roughly ten times the cost of everything else here.
#
# What WAF protects: injection into application logic, credential stuffing
# against auth, abuse of a write path. This distribution serves pre-rendered
# static files from a private S3 bucket over GET and HEAD. There is no
# application logic, no authentication, no form, no database, and no origin that
# can be made to do anything other than return a file that was uploaded at
# deploy time.
#
# The residual risk WAF would address is volumetric abuse inflating the bill.
# That is covered instead by the budget alert in modules/guardrails and by
# CloudFront's own AWS Shield Standard, which is included at no cost.
AWS-0011
